  "findings": [
    "The batched model forward dominates one decode step at every measured batch size; pack and split together are a single digit share of the step at these cache lengths and grow with batch size and cached length, because they copy every layer on every step.",
    "Prefill is one serial forward per request in both strategies, so at high concurrency prefill forwards are a large share of all forwards in a run and are not helped by decode batching at all.",
    "Throughput rises with concurrency while TPOT also rises: one batched forward costs more than one single forward on this CPU, but far less than running the rows one by one, so the gain and the per token cost arrive together.",
    "Padding waste is workload shaped: short prompts of near equal length waste almost nothing, while the mixed workload packs short rows against a longest row about four times their length."
  ],
  "forward_accounting": {
    "note": "Prefill is one serial forward per request in both strategies. At continuous concurrency 8 on the short workload a run has 24 engine steps for 20 requests, so prefill forwards are the large majority of the steps that carry a forward before decode batching can help.",
    "prefill_forwards_per_run": 20,
    "requests_per_run": 20
  },
